# The reference machine's description, for the thermoblock structure.
#
# This is the description the design is established against, and it describes
# the machine named in docs/reference-machine.md rather than the architecture in
# general. Every value carries the origin of that value, because they are not
# all the same kind of fact: two are read off the manufacturer's circuit
# diagram, four are derived from steam tables, and the rest are estimates that
# say what they were estimated from.
#
# Nothing here is measured. The machine has not been on the bench, so no value
# claims it. Commissioning replaces these estimates one at a time, each becoming
# a measured value with its own account, while the rest stand -- which is why
# the origin sits against the value rather than at the top of the file.
#
# Every value also carries the error the design is entitled to assume for it, as
# a fraction of the value, written after the ~ and before the origin. That
# figure is assumed and not measured either: nobody has established how far out
# any of these is, and the fraction is a judgement about how well that kind of
# coefficient is known -- a rating read off a diagram is tighter than a thermal
# mass worked out from a casting nobody has weighed, and a rating that the
# machine's owner disputes is looser than one nobody argues about. Why each
# figure is the size it is, is set out in thermoblock.md; the figures themselves
# are here and only here, so that a coefficient cannot be copied, edited or
# moved without how wrong it may be travelling with it.
#
# A margin sized against these can be checked by somebody else and revisited
# when identification lands better or worse than assumed. A margin sized against
# an uncertainty nobody wrote down can be neither, including by whoever sized it.
#
# What these quantities mean, what they are carried in, and how they relate to
# one another is stated in thermoblock.md beside this file. That statement is
# part of the description; a list of numbers cannot be checked against a machine
# on its own.

ambient_temperature_c = 20.0 ~ 0.25 @estimated Assumed indoor ambient for the machine's AU/NZ domestic market. Nothing has been measured where the machine actually stands, and the value it settles from moves with the room.

brew.thermal_mass_j_per_k = 320.0 ~ 0.4 @estimated From the coffee thermoblock's aluminium casting, taken as roughly 0.35 kg at 900 J/kg/K. The casting has not been weighed and its alloy is not stated in either manual.
brew.heater_power_w = 1000.0 ~ 0.25 @document Coffee thermoblock element, read off the circuit diagram on p.24 of the Sunbeam EM7000 service manual. Disputed: the machine's owner recalls this element as 1200 W and believes the manual describes a variant. The manual is the only citable source, so its figure stands here until the element is measured, which is what settles it -- see the heating section of docs/reference-machine.md, where the concurrency margin that depends on this is worked both ways.
brew.loss_w_per_k = 1.2 ~ 0.6 @estimated From the casting's exposed area under still-air convection, with no allowance for conduction into the chassis. Not measured, and the chassis path is the part most likely to be wrong.
brew.outlet_held_volume_ml = 4.0 ~ 0.6 @estimated The water held between the casting and the group, which the drawn rate has to displace before the group sees the casting's temperature. Worked out from geometry nobody has measured: a channel of roughly 1.8 mm bore taking a few hundred millimetres of path through the casting is about a millilitre, and the run of tube from the block to the group carries the rest. At the two-and-a-bit millilitres per second a shot is actually drawn at, this gives a residence time of order a second and a half -- which is the figure the fixed outlet time constant this coefficient replaces stood for, arrived at from the same block by the same reasoning, so the two accounts agree about the machine rather than only about the number.
brew.outlet_conduction_time_constant_s = 20.0 ~ 0.8 @estimated How fast the held water reaches the casting when nothing is being drawn and conduction is the only path. This is the widest fraction in the file, and it is wide because the figure is not one path but two averaged by hand. Water sitting in the casting's own channel is a millimetre from hot metal and equilibrates in about a second; water already out of the block and in the tube to the group reaches the casting through nothing at all, and on its own would never get there. One constant for both is a judgement about which part of the held volume dominates, and the fraction is sized to admit either answer. Nothing has been measured, and the measurement is the same one the machine cannot take: a temperature at the group.

steam.thermal_mass_j_per_k = 360.0 ~ 0.4 @estimated From the steam thermochamber's casting, taken as slightly heavier than the coffee block's on the strength of the exploded views in the service manual. Neither casting has been weighed.
steam.heater_power_w = 1000.0 ~ 0.12 @document Steam thermoblock element, read off the circuit diagram on p.24 of the Sunbeam EM7000 service manual. The two elements are equally rated.
steam.loss_w_per_k = 1.5 ~ 0.6 @estimated By the same method as the coffee side, and larger because this mass runs far hotter and so drives a larger gradient into the same surroundings.

pump.pressure_bar = 15.0 ~ 0.4 @estimated Taken as the nameplate rating of the Ulka EP5 fitted to the coffee side, recorded as 15 bar in docs/reference-machine.md, which cites no source for that figure. A nameplate rating is what the pump makes against a closed outlet, and this coefficient is what it commands at full duty against the brew path, so using one for the other is a judgement rather than a reading. The pressure reaching the group during an extraction is lower and is not established.
pump.flow_ml_per_s = 7.0 ~ 0.6 @estimated The rate the brew path draws at full pump duty, the asymptote of the flow relation in the same way pump.pressure_bar is the asymptote of the pressure one. Estimated for the type rather than for the part: docs/reference-machine.md records the fitted coffee pump as an Ulka EP5 at 48 W, from the manufacturer's spec sheet for the fitted type, and separately records it as rated 15 bar. Neither is a delivery rate, and no source for one has been found for this pump or its type, so this figure is a judgement rather than a reading. The fraction against it is wide because the span it has to contain is real: a vibratory pump of this class delivers something near this figure into an open path and roughly a third of it through a puck at working pressure, and these equations carry no resistance term to tell those cases apart. It is not the rate a shot is pulled at, and reading it as one would be reading it for the case the model does not represent.
brew.pressure_time_constant_s = 0.8 ~ 0.5 @estimated From comparable vibratory-pump machines of this architecture. The compliance and fill volume of this machine's brew path have not been measured, and this is the coefficient the extraction transient is most sensitive to.

water.feed_temperature_c = 20.0 ~ 0.5 @estimated The temperature the water arrives at. This machine feeds from a tank standing beside it in the room, so the figure is the room's -- and it is the same number as the ambient above for that reason and not because it is the same quantity. The fraction is wide because the two come apart the moment the machine is somewhere else: a plumbed machine on a winter main draws water far below the room it stands in, and this coefficient is where that difference would land. Nothing has been measured; the tank has not been read and no main has been read either.
water.heat_capacity_j_per_ml_k = 4.15 ~ 0.03 @document The energy a millilitre of water takes to raise by one kelvin, from the specific heat and density on the same saturation-line tables the steam figures are read off (IAPWS-IF97). Volumetric rather than per unit mass because the rate this multiplies is a volume per unit time, and there is no density coefficient anywhere in this description to convert with -- folding the density in here keeps the conversion to a single visible number. The fraction covers the spread across the working range rather than any doubt about the tables: the product falls from about 4.18 at room temperature to about 4.05 near boiling, and a single figure has to sit inside that.
water.latent_heat_j_per_ml = 2160.0 ~ 0.05 @document The energy a millilitre of water takes to turn into steam, from the enthalpy of vaporisation and the saturated-liquid density on the same saturation line the other steam figures are read off (IAPWS-IF97): about 2257 J/g at 100 C against a liquid density of about 0.958 g/mL is 2162 J/mL, written here rounded. Volumetric for the reason the heat capacity above is -- the rate it multiplies is a volume per unit time, in millilitres of water turned to vapour rather than of the vapour that results, and there is no density coefficient here to convert with. The fraction covers the span the figure moves over rather than any doubt about the tables: the enthalpy falls to about 2200 J/g by two bar absolute while the liquid density falls with it, and a single figure has to sit inside that.

steam.saturation_temperature_c = 100.0 ~ 0.02 @document Saturation temperature of water at one atmosphere, read off the saturation line of standard steam tables (IAPWS-IF97).
steam.pressure_bar_per_k = 0.036 ~ 0.3 @document Derived from the same saturation line: its slope with temperature just above 100 C is about 3.6 kPa/K. This is a local slope and understates pressure badly well above saturation -- at the 200 C the steam thermostat permits it gives about a quarter of the true figure, so nothing about the steam path's behaviour near that limit can be taken from this model. docs/reference-machine.md notes that its own steam-table figures are worth confirming against a reference before anything is sized from them, and the same applies here.
steam.pressure_fall_bar_per_ml = 0.05 ~ 0.7 @estimated How far a draw carries the steam path's gauge pressure below what the block's temperature alone would give it, per millilitre drawn. Estimated from first principles and from nothing else: a thermoblock holds no reservoir of vapour, so what a draw costs in pressure is set by how fast the block can boil replacement water rather than by any stored volume, and neither rate has been established for this machine or its type. The size follows from the two figures above it -- at the declared slope the path carries about a bar of gauge pressure at the temperature the steam block is run at, and a wand held open for the twenty-odd seconds a jug takes should be well down on that without emptying it, which puts the figure near a twentieth of a bar per millilitre. The fraction is wide because that reasoning fixes an order and not a value: it admits a third of this figure or nearly double it. A gauge on the steam path is the cheapest measurement that would displace it, and until one is fitted nothing about how far the steam side sags under a draw should be taken from this model.
